  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret a periodic continuous-time signal as a weighted sum of sinusoidal harmonic components organized around a fundamental frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A periodic continuous-time signal can be read as a weighted sum of sinusoidal harmonic components organized around a fundamental frequency. The constant part is the DC term, and the oscillating parts sit at frequencies $k\\omega_0$ with weights that tell how much of each harmonic is present.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+A_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+B_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the smallest usable case: one constant offset plus the first harmonic at $\\omega_0$. Read the constant term as the DC component and read the sinusoidal terms as the first weighted harmonic contribution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[A_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+B_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Higher harmonics follow the same pattern at $2\\omega_0$, $3\\omega_0$, and beyond. The next sections will use this lens to identify one component, read its weight, and then interpret a full harmonic sum as one periodic waveform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying One Sinusoidal Component in a Periodic Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=3+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$, identify one sinusoidal component and state which harmonic it is.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A single sinusoidal component is identified by its frequency $k\\omega_0$, not by its coefficient alone. In a harmonic-sum description, the DC term sits separately and each sinusoid is tagged by its harmonic frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+A_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+B_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+A_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply that rule to $x(t)=3+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$. The DC term is $3$, so the first harmonic sinusoidal component is $2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That component has harmonic index $k=1$, so its frequency is $\\omega_0$. The coefficient $2$ is the weight on that harmonic, while the term $-\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$ is the second harmonic at $2\\omega_0$. Read the frequency first, then name the term it identifies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Weights on Sinusoidal Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=1+\\tfrac{3}{2}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$, explain what the weight on each sinusoidal component says about its contribution.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a harmonic-sum description, each coefficient is the weight on its sinusoidal component. Compare the weights by magnitude first; the sign can change orientation or phase, but it does not change the fact that the term is present.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+A_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+B_1\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+A_3\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply that rule to $x(t)=1+\\tfrac{3}{2}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$. The weights have magnitudes $\\tfrac{3}{2}$, $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, and $4$, so the $3\\omega_0$ term is the strongest contribution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1+\\tfrac{3}{2}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient $4$ is the largest magnitude, so $4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$ contributes most strongly. The minus sign on $-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$ changes the term's orientation, but it is still a smaller contribution than the $3\\omega_0$ harmonic. Read coefficient magnitude as weight and keep the harmonic frequency fixed while comparing terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Sum of Harmonic Sinusoids",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=2+\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+\\tfrac{3}{4}\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$, interpret the signal as a sum of harmonic sinusoids and identify which frequencies appear.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the whole expression as one periodic signal. The constant term $2$ is the DC part, and the remaining terms sit at harmonic frequencies of the same fundamental spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+A_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+B_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+A_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+B_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply that grouping to $x(t)=2+\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+\\tfrac{3}{4}\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$. The terms at $\\omega_0$ are $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, and the next harmonic is $\\tfrac{3}{4}\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2+\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+\\tfrac{3}{4}\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal contains frequency components at $0$, $\\omega_0$, and $2\\omega_0$. Read the full sum as one periodic waveform built from the DC term and integer multiples of the fundamental frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Periodic Signals as Sums of Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Relating a Sinusoidal Sum to the Periodic Waveform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given a harmonic sum such as $x(t)=1+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$, describe the waveform features implied by the DC term and the harmonic weights.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the sum as the waveform description itself. The constant term $1$ sets the vertical offset, and the largest harmonic term usually controls the main oscillation you notice first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A_0+A_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+B_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply that rule to $x(t)=1+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$. The waveform is centered at $1$, the $2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ term gives the strongest up-and-down motion, and the $2\\omega_0$ term adds a smaller distortion to the shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is one periodic waveform, not separate pieces. It repeats with the same fundamental spacing, but the second harmonic makes the shape less purely sinusoidal than $2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ alone.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
